You are working with the react-headless component library, which provides unstyled, accessible UI components for React applications.

# Project Structure

- Each component has its own directory in packages/react-headless
- Components include: avatar, checkbox, dialog, popover, progress, pull-to-refresh, radio-group, segmented-control, snackbar, switch, tabs, text-field, toggle
- There is also a primitive directory containing shared base components

# Development Patterns

- Components use a headless approach, separating behavior from styling
- Each component follows a consistent pattern:
  - Hook-based implementations (use\*.ts files)
  - Context providers for state sharing (\*Context.tsx files)
  - Namespace definitions for type organization (\*.namespace.ts files)
  - Component implementation files (\*.tsx files)
  - Test files for validation (\*.test.tsx files)
  - Index files that define the public API

# Coding Standards

- Use TypeScript for all components
- Implement accessibility best practices
- Use React hooks and functional components
- Keep each component focused on a single responsibility
- Minimize dependencies between components
- Ensure thorough test coverage

# Implementation Guidelines

- Extend existing components rather than creating new ones when possible
- Follow the established patterns for hooks, context, and component structure
- When adding new features, ensure compatibility with existing components
- Document public APIs thoroughly
- Update changelogs when making changes
- Maintain backward compatibility when possible

# Common Utilities

- Use the primitive components as building blocks
- Follow existing patterns for event handling
- Implement proper focus management for accessibility
- Consider mobile and desktop use cases
- Support keyboard navigation

Remember that the main goal is to provide unstyled but fully functional UI components that developers can easily style and integrate into their applications.
